27863c6b [gemini-3.8-flash] #Your storage configuration is clean, modern, and well-proportioned for an Arch Linux installation.
27863c6b [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27863c6b [gemini-3.8-flash] #A few observations on the layout:
27863c6b [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27863c6b [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Drive Allocation: You have two NVMe drives. Using the larger 1 TB drive (`nvme1n1`) for
27863c6b [gemini-3.8-flash] #the base system and dedicating the 500 GB drive (`nvme0n1`) to a dedicated mount point
27863c6b [gemini-3.8-flash] #(`/mnt/fast`) is a practical way to isolate high-throughput tasks, games, virtual machines, or
27863c6b [gemini-3.8-flash] #scratch space.
27863c6b [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Boot Partition: The 1 GB `/boot` partition provides ample headroom for multiple kernels,
27863c6b [gemini-3.8-flash] #fallback initramfs images, and microcode updates without running into space constraints.
27863c6b [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Root Partition: Allocating the remainder of the drive to `/` avoids artificial boundaries
27863c6b [gemini-3.8-flash] #between `/home` and system files, keeping maintenance straightforward.
27863c6b [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Swap: The 15.3 GB `zram0` device indicates that compressed in-RAM swap is active, which
27863c6c [gemini-3.8-flash] #reduces wear on the NVMe drives and provides faster paging performance compared to a standard swap
27863c6c [gemini-3.8-flash] #partition or file.
27863c6c [gemini-3.8-flash] #
27863c6c [gemini-3.8-flash] #If you are looking to review specific aspects such as filesystem choices, mount options, TRIM
