279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪣 0.7° $0.047 178.5KB 8.41s
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #There is currently no official or widely adopted "nano" clone package in the Deno standard library
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #or JSR ecosystem.
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #A few community experiments and ports exist—primarily TypeScript ports of Salvatore Sanfilippo’s
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #(antirez) Kilo editor, which is the classic ~1000-line C baseline that nano-style minimal
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #editors are built on. However, none have achieved the status of an established, maintained utility
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #library in Deno.
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #Most CLI tools in the Deno and Node ecosystems handle editing by either:
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #1. Delegating to `$EDITOR`: Spawning `nano`, `micro`, or `vim` via `Deno.Command` with
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #inherited stdio.
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #2. Building a custom lightweight visual editor: Since `slopprompt.ts` already sets up raw
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #mode and basic key reading, implementing a self-contained Kilo/nano clone in pure TypeScript takes
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #roughly 300–400 lines.
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #The architecture for a native Deno nano-style editor typically consists of:
279e0a62 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• An alternate screen buffer context (`\x1b[?1049h` on enter, `\x1b[?1049l` on exit).
279e0a63 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• A line buffer (`string[]`) with cursor tracking (`cx`, `cy`) and viewport offsets (`rowOffset`,
279e0a63 [gemini-3.8-flash] #`colOffset`).
279e0a63 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• A unified keyboard reader parsing arrow keys, backspace, page navigation, and control chords
279e0a63 [gemini-3.8-flash] #(such as `Ctrl+O` for write-out and `Ctrl+X` for exit).
279e0a63 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• A render loop drawing visible rows, a two-line nano-style shortcut bar at the bottom, and
279e0a63 [gemini-3.8-flash] #positioning the cursor.
279e0a63 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
279e0a63 [gemini-3.8-flash] #Would you like to explore writing a self-contained visual editor module, or look into invoking the
279e0a63 [gemini-3.8-flash] #system's `$EDITOR`?
279e0a63 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
