28b36b7b [gemini-3.8-flash] #In Linux, `mount` attaches a storage device's filesystem to a specific directory in your
28b36b7b [gemini-3.8-flash] #system tree, while `umount` (unmount) safely detaches it.
28b36b7b [gemini-3.8-flash] #
28b36b7b [gemini-3.8-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
28b36b7b [gemini-3.8-flash] #
28b36b7b [gemini-3.8-flash] #`mount`
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #• What it does: Connects a storage partition (e.g., `/dev/sda1`) to a folder (e.g.,
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #`/mnt/storage`), making the files accessible.
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Analogy: Plugging a drive in and opening the door to read or write files.
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Usage:
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #  sudo mount /dev/sda1 /mnt/storage
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #
28b36b7c [gemini-3.8-flash] #
