28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.008 3.960KB 6.85s
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #On Arch Linux, Nginx logs are typically located at:
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #• Default log paths:*
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #/var/log/nginx/access.log
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #/var/log/nginx/error.log
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #• Access log* — records every request (IP, timestamp, URL, status code, user agent, etc.)
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #• Error log* — records errors, warnings, and startup/shutdown messages.
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #• Checking your actual config:*
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #The paths can be customized. Check your config to confirm:
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #grep -r "access_log\|error_log" /etc/nginx/
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #Common places these directives appear:
28bc8a4a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #- `/etc/nginx/nginx.conf` — global defaults
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #- `/etc/nginx/sites-available/` or `/etc/nginx/conf.d/` — per-site overrides (some setups
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #define separate logs per virtual host, e.g. `/var/log/nginx/example.com.access.log`)
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #• Live tailing logs:*
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #sudo tail -f /var/log/nginx/access.log
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #sudo tail -f /var/log/nginx/error.log
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #• If using systemd* (Arch uses systemd by default), you can also check the service's own
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #journal output:
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #journalctl -u nginx -f
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #This is useful for startup errors that might occur before the log files are even created, or if
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #Nginx fails to write to disk for permission reasons.
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #• Permission note:* If Nginx isn't writing logs, check that the `nginx` user (or whichever
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #user it runs as, per `user` directive in `nginx.conf`) has write access to `/var/log/nginx/`.
28bc8a4b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc9090 [skid@archlinux] is that a 404 i see, the actual payload is .json.gz
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.013 5.547KB 11.19s
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #Yeah, that's a classic case — if the client requests `something.json` but only `something.json.gz`
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #exists on disk, Nginx will return a 404 unless it's explicitly configured to handle that.
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #• Why this happens:*
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #Nginx doesn't automatically look for a `.gz` version of a file unless told to. It just checks for
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #the literal path being served.
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #• Fix — use `gzip_static`:*
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #If you're pre-compressing files (common for static JSON/JS assets), enable the `gzip_static` module
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #in your server or location block:
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #location /path/to/data/ {
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #	gzip_static on;
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #}
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #This tells Nginx: "If a client requests `foo.json` and `foo.json.gz` exists, serve the gzip version
28bc914a [claude-sonnet-5] #with the correct `Content-Encoding: gzip` header — as long as the client's `Accept-Encoding`
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #supports gzip."
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #• Requirements:*
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #- `gzip_static` is a separate module — check if it's compiled in:
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #  nginx -V 2>&1 | grep -o with-http_gzip_static_module
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #  On Arch, the official `nginx` package usually includes it by default. If not, `nginx-mainline`
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #from AUR or official repos includes it too — check with the command above to confirm before
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #assuming.
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #- The `.json.gz` file must sit next to the original `.json` file (or in place of it), matching the
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #same base name.
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #- The client must send `Accept-Encoding: gzip` (virtually all browsers/curl do by default).
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #• Quick test:*
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #curl -I -H "Accept-Encoding: gzip" http://yourserver/path/to/file.json
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #Look for:
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #Content-Encoding: gzip
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914b [claude-sonnet-5] #• If you don't have the module or don't want to rely on it:*
28bc914c [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914c [claude-sonnet-5] #Alternative is an internal rewrite/try_files fallback:
28bc914c [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914c [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914c [claude-sonnet-5] #location / {
28bc914c [claude-sonnet-5] #	try_files $uri $uri.gz =404;
28bc914c [claude-sonnet-5] #}
28bc914c [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914c [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914c [claude-sonnet-5] #But note: this serves the raw gzip bytes without setting `Content-Encoding: gzip`, so the client
28bc914c [claude-sonnet-5] #will get garbled data unless you also set the header manually — `gzip_static` is the cleaner
28bc914c [claude-sonnet-5] #solution.
28bc914c [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bc914c [claude-sonnet-5] #Want to paste your relevant `server` / `location` block? I can point out exactly what to change.
28bc914c [claude-sonnet-5] #
