28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #Interesting — all permissions and ownership look completely fine there. Every directory is `755`
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #and traversable, and the file itself is readable. So it's not a filesystem/permissions
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #issue.
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #This points to a different, very common `gzip_static` gotcha:
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #• `gzip_static` only serves the `.gz` file if the client's request includes `Accept-Encoding:
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #gzip`. If it doesn't, and no plain (uncompressed) `.json` file exists, Nginx returns 404 instead of
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #falling back.*
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #So the question becomes: is the actual browser request sending `Accept-Encoding: gzip` for that
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #particular request? Most browsers do by default, but it's worth confirming directly.
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #• Test it explicitly:*
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabab [claude-sonnet-5] ## Without Accept-Encoding (simulates client not accepting gzip)
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #curl -I https://skid.nz/roagrid/transit/1/3420/11559.json
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] ## With Accept-Encoding: gzip
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #curl -I -H "Accept-Encoding: gzip" https://skid.nz/roagrid/transit/1/3420/11559.json
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #If the first returns `404` and the second returns `200` with `Content-Encoding: gzip`, that
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #confirms the theory.
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #• The fix — add `gunzip on;`*
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #This tells nginx to decompress the `.gz` file on-the-fly for clients that don't request
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #gzip, instead of 404ing:
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #location /roagrid/ {
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #	add_header Access-Control-Allow-Origin *;
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #	add_header Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable";
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #	root /mnt/grid26;
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #	gzip_static on;
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #	gunzip on;
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #}
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #This requires the `http_gunzip_module` — check it's available:
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #nginx -V 2>&1 | grep -o http_gunzip_module
28bcabac [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabad [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabad [claude-sonnet-5] #If that's compiled in, adding `gunzip on;` to all three of your `roagrid`, `topogrid` (and
28bcabad [claude-sonnet-5] #optionally `aerialgrid` if you ever add compressed files there) location blocks should resolve the
28bcabad [claude-sonnet-5] #404 regardless of whether the client sends `Accept-Encoding: gzip`.
28bcabad [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bcabad [claude-sonnet-5] #Try the curl tests first to confirm the diagnosis before changing config — that'll tell us for
28bcabad [claude-sonnet-5] #certain.
