28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #Yes, you can raise the open file descriptor limit on Arch. Errno 24 (EMFILE) means your process hit
28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #its per-process fd limit, not necessarily a system-wide one, so where you set this depends on how
28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #the service is launched.
28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #• Check current limits first:*
28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #ulimit -Sn
28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #ulimit -Hn
28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #cat /proc/sys/fs/file-max
28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #• If this runs as a systemd service* (likely, given the log format), ulimit changes in your
28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #shell won't affect it — you need to set it in the unit file:
28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cc [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #systemctl edit your-service.service
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #Add:
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #[Service]
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #LimitNOFILE=65536
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #Then:
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #systemctl daemon-reload
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #systemctl restart your-service.service
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #Verify it took effect:
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #cat /proc/$(pidof your_binary)/limits | grep "Max open files"
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #• If launched from a shell/login session*, edit `/etc/security/limits.conf`:
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #your_user    soft    nofile    65536
28bd72cd [claude-sonnet-5] #your_user    hard    nofile    65536
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #This requires PAM's `pam_limits` module to be active in the relevant PAM config (usually already
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #the case on Arch), and a fresh login session.
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #• System-wide ceiling* (rarely the bottleneck unless you're running many processes with
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #high per-process limits):
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #sysctl fs.file-max
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #To persist a higher value, add to `/etc/sysctl.d/99-filemax.conf`:
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #fs.file-max = 2097152
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #Then:
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #sysctl --system
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #• One thing worth checking before just raising limits:* if this server is legitimately
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #holding thousands of concurrent connections/files open, raising the limit is the right fix. But if
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #fd usage climbs over time under normal load, it could be a descriptor leak — e.g., file handles
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #from `loadString` not being closed on error paths or after serving the response. Worth checking
28bd72ce [claude-sonnet-5] #with:
28bd72cf [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cf [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cf [claude-sonnet-5] #ls -l /proc/$(pidof your_binary)/fd | wc -l
28bd72cf [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cf [claude-sonnet-5] #
28bd72cf [claude-sonnet-5] #If that count grows steadily over time even without more incoming requests, it's a leak rather than
28bd72cf [claude-sonnet-5] #a capacity problem.
28bd72cf [claude-sonnet-5] #
