28dc68f2 [claude-sonnet-5] #Two Issues Here
28dc68f2 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f2 [claude-sonnet-5] #Issue 1: The Real Error — Dependency Conflict
28dc68f2 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f2 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f2 [claude-sonnet-5] #error: removing falkon breaks dependency 'falkon' required by kde-network-meta
28dc68f2 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f2 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #Falkon is currently required by the `kde-network-meta` metapackage (a bundling package that
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #pulls in a group of network-related apps as part of the Plasma "network" component group). Pacman
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #won't remove a package while another package still depends on it.
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #Fix: Remove Both Together
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #Since `kde-network-meta` is just a metapackage (no real files of its own, just dependency links),
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #it's safe to remove alongside Falkon:
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #sudo pacman -Rns falkon kde-network-meta
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #This won't break your KDE desktop — metapackages exist only to group installs, not to provide
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #functionality themselves.
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #Alternative: Force Remove (Skip Dependency Check)
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #If you specifically want to keep `kde-network-meta` installed for some reason (not usually
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #necessary):
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #sudo pacman -Rdd falkon
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #⚠️ Use this cautiously — `-dd` skips all dependency checks, so only do this if you're sure
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #it won't break anything else.
28dc68f3 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #Issue 2: That Weird `^[[200~` Text
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #bash: $'\E[200~sudo': command not found
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #This is a bracketed paste mode artifact. It happens when you paste text into the terminal
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #and your terminal emulator wraps it in special escape codes (`\E[200~` ... `\E[201~`) to tell bash
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #"this is pasted text, not typed." Sometimes bash/terminal combos mishandle this, and the raw escape
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #codes leak into the command line.
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #Quick Fix: Disable Bracketed Paste in Bash
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #Add this to your `~/.bashrc`:
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #bind 'set enable-bracketed-paste off'
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #Then reload:
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #source ~/.bashrc
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f4 [claude-sonnet-5] #Alternative: Just Retype Instead of Pasting
28dc68f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dc68f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #If this only happens occasionally, simply typing the command manually (like you did successfully
28dc68f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #the second time) avoids the issue entirely.
28dc68f5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
