28dcf65d [claude-sonnet-5] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.029 15.22KB 19.38s
28dcf65d [claude-sonnet-5] #Good question to raise — yes, there are a few things worth being aware of if you're moving
28dcf65d [claude-sonnet-5] #pixel-drawing work into Web Workers, especially since you're already dealing with CORS/tainting.
28dcf65d [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65d [claude-sonnet-5] #1. Workers Don't Have `Image()` or Direct DOM Access
28dcf65d [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #Inside a worker, you can't use `new Image()` — there's no DOM. Instead, you use `fetch()` +
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #`createImageBitmap()`:
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #async function loadImageBitmap(url) {
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #	const response = await fetch(url, { mode: "cors" });
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #	const blob = await response.blob();
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #	return createImageBitmap(blob);
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #}
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #2. CORS Rules Still Apply — Same Tainting Logic
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #Even in a worker, if the fetch doesn't have proper CORS headers, the resulting `ImageBitmap` can
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #still be tainted when drawn to an `OffscreenCanvas`. The `mode: "cors"` option on `fetch()`
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #is the worker-equivalent of setting `img.crossOrigin = "anonymous"` on the main thread.
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #function drawInWorker(bitmap, canvas) {
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #	const ctx = canvas.getContext("2d");
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #	ctx.drawImage(bitmap, 0, 0);
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #	// Still throws if tainted, same as main thread:
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #	const pixels = ctx.getImageData(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #	return pixels;
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #}
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65e [claude-sonnet-5] #3. `OffscreenCanvas` Support
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #If you're rendering inside the worker itself (rather than just processing data), you'll want
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #`OffscreenCanvas`, transferred from the main thread:
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #// Main thread
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #const canvas = document.querySelector("canvas");
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #const offscreen = canvas.transferControlToOffscreen();
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #worker.postMessage({ canvas: offscreen }, [offscreen]);
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #// worker.js
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #self.onmessage = (e) => {
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #	const canvas = e.data.canvas;
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #	const ctx = canvas.getContext("2d");
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #	// draw as normal
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #};
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #Support is solid in modern Chromium and Firefox; Safari lagged behind for a while but has caught up
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #in recent versions — worth checking your minimum target browser if "cross-platform" includes older
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #Safari/WebKit-based environments (e.g., embedded webviews).
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #4. Transferring Data Back to Main Thread
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #Pixel data (`ImageData`, `ArrayBuffer`, `ImageBitmap`) should be sent using transferable
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #objects, not structured-cloned copies — this avoids expensive memory duplication:
28dcf65f [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #// Worker side
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #self.postMessage({ imageData }, [imageData.data.buffer]);
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #5. If You Ever Want `SharedArrayBuffer` (High-Performance Pixel Sharing)
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #This requires your server to send cross-origin isolation headers:
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #This is a stricter requirement than plain CORS and would also apply to your CDN-served images —
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #likely unnecessary unless you're doing heavy real-time pixel manipulation across threads.
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #• Bottom line: Web Workers don't introduce new tainting rules — they enforce
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #the same* CORS rules you already fixed, just via `fetch(mode: "cors")` instead of
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #`img.crossOrigin`. The main things to watch are (a) using `createImageBitmap` instead of `Image`,
28dcf660 [claude-sonnet-5] #and (b) transferable objects for performance.
