29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 gpt-5.6-sol 🌀 🪠 🧊 $0.037 20.53KB 9.88s
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #• Code - OSS is the open-source base of Visual Studio Code*.
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #Microsoft association
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #- The source project is primarily developed by Microsoft and community contributors.
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #- It is published at [`microsoft/vscode`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode) under the MIT
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #licence.
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #- Microsoft builds that source into the branded Visual Studio Code product, adding
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #Microsoft branding, distribution terms, telemetry configuration, and access to Microsoft’s
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #extension marketplace.
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #- Arch Linux’s `code` package is generally an independently compiled Code - OSS build
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #rather than Microsoft’s official binary.
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #Your version information therefore looks like an Arch-packaged build of the VS Code source.
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #`ElectronBuildId: undefined` is usually only a packaging detail.
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #The listed `@github/copilot` components indicate that Copilot support is installed or bundled in
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #that build. GitHub is owned by Microsoft, but its presence does not turn Code - OSS into the
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #official Microsoft VS Code distribution.
29060059 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
2906005a [gpt-5.6-sol] #You can check which Arch package supplied it with:
2906005a [gpt-5.6-sol] #
2906005a [gpt-5.6-sol] #
2906005a [gpt-5.6-sol] #pacman -Qo "$(command -v code)"
2906005a [gpt-5.6-sol] #
2906005a [gpt-5.6-sol] #
2906005a [gpt-5.6-sol] #Typically:
2906005a [gpt-5.6-sol] #
2906005a [gpt-5.6-sol] #- `code` — Arch’s Code - OSS build
2906005a [gpt-5.6-sol] #- `visual-studio-code-bin` — Microsoft’s official branded build, commonly installed from the AUR
2906005a [gpt-5.6-sol] #- `vscodium` — community build intended to remove Microsoft branding and telemetry defaults
2906005a [gpt-5.6-sol] #
2906044c [skid@archlinux] if i wish to brand similar to way microsoft do it, should i look at what level, arch distro? or below that? 
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 gpt-5.6-sol 🌀 🪠 🧊 $0.047 22.17KB 15.96s
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #Brand it at the application/source-build level, below the Arch distribution packaging
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #layer.
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #A sensible structure is:
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #1. Fork `microsoft/vscode`
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #	- Keep your product-specific changes and update strategy there.
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #	- The source is MIT-licensed, but Microsoft names, logos, and other trademarks are separate.
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #2. Configure the product identity
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #	- Edit the build’s `product.json` values: product name, application name, data-directory name, URL
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #protocol, server names, and related identifiers.
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #	- Replace icons, splash/branding assets, desktop-entry details, and About-page information.
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #	- Use unique application and data-directory names so it can coexist with Code - OSS and VS Code.
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #3. Choose your services
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #	- Configure your own update mechanism, telemetry policy, documentation URLs, issue tracker, and
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #crash-reporting services.
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #	- Do not assume that a custom distribution may use Microsoft’s Visual Studio Marketplace. Many
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #community builds use Open VSX, subject to its own terms.
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #	- Copilot and other Microsoft/GitHub services have their own licensing and distribution
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #conditions.
29060555 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #4. Package it for Arch
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #	- Use a `PKGBUILD` to build/install your branded application.
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #	- The Arch package should mainly handle dependencies, installation paths, desktop entries, icons,
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #MIME handlers, and package metadata.
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #	- Avoid making the Arch packaging scripts the sole location for substantial product changes unless
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #it is only a small personal build.
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #So the layers are roughly:
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #• Code - OSS source → your branded build → Arch package*
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #For a quick personal experiment, applying patches from a custom `PKGBUILD` is reasonable. For a
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #product you intend to maintain or distribute, keep the branding in a source fork/build
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #configuration and let the Arch package merely package the resulting product.
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #Also choose names and artwork that cannot be mistaken for Visual Studio Code, and retain
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #the MIT licence and applicable third-party notices when distributing it.
29060556 [gpt-5.6-sol] #
