29198105 [gemini-3.8-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.003 623B 11.45s
29198105 [gemini-3.8-flash] #Yes, you can extract a significant amount of additional status information to give you better
29198105 [gemini-3.8-flash] #visibility into what is happening.
29198105 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
29198105 [gemini-3.8-flash] #Assuming you are using the Bunny Ruby library for RabbitMQ, you can inspect details such as:
29198105 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
29198105 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Connection details: Verify the active host, port, heartbeat intervals, and whether TLS is
29198105 [gemini-3.8-flash] #enabled.
29198105 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Server information: Retrieve the broker's version, cluster name, and supported
29198105 [gemini-3.8-flash] #capabilities directly from the connection's server properties.
29198105 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Channel status: Check channel health, the channel ID number, and whether it is actively
29198105 [gemini-3.8-flash] #open.
29198106 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Queue statistics: When declaring or querying a queue, you can retrieve the current
29198106 [gemini-3.8-flash] #message count and the number of active consumers attached to it.
29198106 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
29198106 [gemini-3.8-flash] #If your script is using the Bunny library for Ruby (or if you are working with a different
29198106 [gemini-3.8-flash] #platform, such as the Bunny.net API), please let me know. If you would like to see an updated
29198106 [gemini-3.8-flash] #script with these status checks implemented, feel free to ask and I will be happy to provide one.
29198106 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.004 2.996KB 6.41s
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #Thank you for sharing the script. That clarifies that you are interacting with the Bunny.net CDN
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #Purge API rather than RabbitMQ.
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #The Bunny.net purge endpoint typically returns a simple response, but you can extract and display
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #several useful status metrics:
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Round-Trip Latency: Measuring the elapsed time between dispatching the request and
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #receiving the response using `performance.now()`.
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Detailed HTTP Response Info: Inspecting the exact status code (`response.status`), status
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #text (`response.statusText`), and tracking identifiers (such as the `CDN-RequestCountryCode` or
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #server headers, if returned).
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Response Body Content: While a standard purge often returns an empty body or a simple
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #boolean/JSON acknowledgement, parsing and printing the raw body text or structured JSON can verify
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #the exact confirmation message returned by Bunny.net.
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #• Timestamp Logging: Emitting an ISO timestamp indicating exactly when the cache
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #invalidation completed.
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #If you would like me to provide an updated version of the script incorporating these status details
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #using named helper functions and tabs, please let me know.
291981a2 [gemini-3.8-flash] #
