2950e3ba [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.003 1.947KB 11.53s
2950e3ba [deepseek-flash] #A few observations, mostly small.
2950e3ba [deepseek-flash] #
2950e3ba [deepseek-flash] #• Correct as written*
2950e3ba [deepseek-flash] #
2950e3ba [deepseek-flash] #- Resolving each pattern against the zone with `new URL(pattern, base)` is sound, and the trailing
2950e3ba [deepseek-flash] #slash on `purgeZone` is required for it to behave as a base rather than a file path. `*` is not in
2950e3ba [deepseek-flash] #the path or form-urlencoded percent-encode sets, so it survives both the URL parse and the
2950e3ba [deepseek-flash] #`searchParams` serialisation intact — the wildcard reaches Bunny unescaped.
2950e3ba [deepseek-flash] #- The `AccessKey` header name and the `url`/`async` query parameters match Bunny's purge endpoint.
2950e3ba [deepseek-flash] #- Sequential `await` in the loop is fine, and `async=false` means each call blocks until the purge
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #completes, so ordering is deterministic.
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #• Difference worth confirming*
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #The commented-out patterns at the top were `dsp`, `wire`, `dsptool.css`, but the live
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #`patterns` array is `nitro`, `wire`, `dsptool.css`. If `dsp` → `nitro` was the
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #intended edit, fine; if it was a scratch change, the old `dsp*` set is now unpurged.
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #• Robustness*
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #- `./apikey` resolves against the process working directory, not the module. Running the script
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #from elsewhere will fail with a confusing `NotFound`. Anchoring it to `import.meta.url` (or
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #`import.meta.dirname`) makes it location-independent.
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #- Nothing catches a mid-loop failure, so a single non-OK response aborts the remaining patterns
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #with a partial purge. That's a defensible fail-fast choice, but if all three need to be attempted,
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #collect failures and report at the end.
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #- If you purge often enough to hit Bunny's rate limiting, a short delay between calls (or retry on
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #429) would help. I don't want to quote a specific limit since it may have changed.
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #- The error path reads the body as text; Bunny typically returns JSON, so if you want to
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #distinguish validation errors from transient ones, parse it.
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #• Style*
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #- `): Promise<void>{` is missing the space before the brace that the rest of the file uses.
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #- The commented-out pattern lines are now dead and duplicated by the array; worth deleting.
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #- The function takes the API key per call. It's only ever called with the same value, so a
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #module-level constant or a closure over the key would read more cleanly.
2950e3bb [deepseek-flash] #
