2986fbe3 [roha]    worker can't do it after the fact. ## Why the worker can't do it post-hoc `Bumpmap.post()`
2986fbe3 [roha]    transfers the buffers away: ```js const transfer=[topo.buffer,diff.buffer,ground.buffer];
2986fbe3 [roha]    postMessage(message,transfer); ``` After that the worker's `topo`/`diff`/`ground` are
2986fbe3 [roha]    detached. Even if you sent a `dig` command later, there's nothing to dig into. So "post once
2986fbe3 [roha]    footprints are dug" only works if the dig happens in the worker, before the transfer. ## What
2986fbe4 [roha]    that requires Three things move to the worker side: - **`Bits`** from `wire_util.js` — the
2986fbe4 [roha]    polygon rasteriser. Currently only loaded on the main thread. - **`TopoTile.digOutline`** — or
2986fbe4 [roha]    a shared version of it. It's ~30 lines and reads only from the heightmap and the outline. -
2986fbe4 [roha]    **A protocol change** — the worker must not `post()` until it has received the roof outlines.
2986fbe4 [roha]    And one thing has to be reordered on the main thread: `onDrawingResponse` must forward the
2986fbe4 [roha]    roof outlines to the worker, and `createTopo()` in the worker must defer its `postFlora` +
2986fbe4 [roha]    `post` until those arrive. ## New flow ``` main worker ──── ────── fetchTileRaw ──heightmap──▶
2986fbe4 [roha]    accumulate fetchDrawing (hold, don't post yet) onDrawingResponse extract ROOF outlines
2986fbe4 [roha]    ──dig───────▶ apply dig to topo/diff/ground postFlora(256,8,FloraPen,22) post({bump, flora})
2986fbe4 [roha]    ──▶ main (transfer buffers now) ``` Because the dig runs first, both the bump surface and the
2986fbe4 [roha]    flora surface are built from the same dug state. The main thread's `onBump` creates
2986fbe4 [roha]    `topoSurface` from an already-dug heightmap — no rebuild in `meshTile` needed. The whole
