2986fbf8 [roha]    Looking at the path, the order isn't chosen anywhere — it falls out of *when* a tile finishes
2986fbf8 [roha]    loading. ## How the order is actually determined `refreshTiles` hands tiles to `populateHome`
2986fbf8 [roha]    in scan order: ```js for(let a=-r;a<=r;a++){ for(let b=-r;b<=r;b++){ if(a*a+b*b<=r*r){ const
2986fbf8 [roha]    tile=this.tileGet(tx+a,ty+b); if(tile.fetched){ if(tile.ready){ visible.push(tile); ``` But
2986fbf8 [roha]    `visible` only contains tiles that are `fetched && ready`, and tiles become ready
2986fbf8 [roha]    asynchronously. A tile is meshed on the first frame it appears there, and `meshTile` appends
2986fbf8 [roha]    its shapes to the layer: ```js tile.staticmeesh=layer0.addStaticShapes(surfaces,...);
2986fbf8 [roha]    tile.tilemesh=layer0.addTileShape(topo,...); ``` `drawLayerList` then walks `layer.shapes` in
2986fbf8 [roha]    that array order, so **draw order == mesh order == order of load completion**. And load order
2986fbf8 [roha]    is nearest-first, because the fetch priority is a distance: ```js tile.priority=1-(a*a+b*b);
2986fbf8 [roha]    this.fetchTile(tile); ``` `compareTiles` sorts ascending, `fetchTile` pops from the end, so
2986fbf8 [roha]    the closest tile is fetched and meshed first. The tile layer is therefore drawn
2986fbf8 [roha]    **front-to-back**. ## Why that's visible For an opaque, depth-tested scene, front-to-back is
2986fbf8 [roha]    fine — it's even the fast path. It only bites where geometry is blended or coincident: -
2986fbf9 [roha]    Terrain alpha is `O=0.94`, and `WebGL2Context.clear` enables `BLEND` globally with depth
2986fbf9 [roha]    writes on. So each tile is 94% opaque and blended. Where adjacent tiles overlap at the seam,
2986fbf9 [roha]    the second tile blends over the first, and how much darkening you see depends on which was
2986fbf9 [roha]    drawn first. Front-to-back makes it worse, and it changes as tiles stream in. - Flora is
2986fbf9 [roha]    genuinely transparent (`A=0.52`), so it needs strict back-to-front. Front-to-back guarantees
2986fbf9 [roha]    the wrong blend. - Coincident surfaces (walls/coast on terrain) resolve by
